<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- THE TESTS THAT READ A REAL MACHINE, AND THE ONLY PROJECT ALLOWED TO.
       The Resource Monitor mission (issue #2811) needs tests that take real readings from a real
       machine: it is the one thing a resource probe exists to do, and no fake can prove it.

       IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A GATE SUITE. scripts\test-local.ps1 names every project the gate runs -
       default, installer and parked - and this project is in none of those lists, on purpose. That is
       what the owner's ruling of 11 October 2026 requires: a test in a gate suite never lets a real
       disk, a real clock, a real process or a sleep decide whether it passes, because a sensor that
       lights at random is worse than no sensor. The source guard
       GateTestsDoNotDependOnTheMachineTests reads its suite list out of that same script, so a project
       the gate does not run is not scanned - and must never be added to those lists.

       TWO LOCKS, NOT ONE. Being outside the gate keeps these tests from running there at all. On top of
       that every test carries [LiveMachineFact], so even run directly they SKIP - visibly, with a
       reason - unless CC_DIRECTOR_LIVE_MACHINE_TESTS=1 is set. A machine-reading test that runs by
       accident is the thing being prevented, so it is prevented twice.

       WHAT DOES RUN IT, said plainly so a skipped test here surprises nobody. It is in cc-director.sln,
       so two things load this assembly: the solution build, which is the point - a signature change that
       breaks these tests breaks the build like any other - and the scheduled full continuous integration
       run on main, which runs "dotnet test cc-director.sln" every two hours when main has moved. That run
       executes this project. Today that means its handful of attribute tests pass there and every live
       test SKIPS, because the variable is absent on that runner; a derived contract class will therefore
       show up as skipped in the full run, which is correct and is not a gap. The same is true of
       CcDirector.Core.GitTests, which says so in its own project file. What runs the live tests for real
       is a person or a session asking for them: the Developer proving a platform probe, and the
       cross-platform quality assurance run.

       HOW TO RUN IT, on the platform whose probe you are proving:
         CC_DIRECTOR_LIVE_MACHINE_TESTS=1 dotnet test src/CcDirector.Core.MachineLiveTests -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- Shares the namespace with the other Core test projects so a moved file needs no edit. -->
    <RootNamespace>CcDirector.Core.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.*" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.*" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CcDirector.Core\CcDirector.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The contract checks have ONE definition, in the parked half beside the other shared test
         support, and are linked here and into CcDirector.Core.UnitTests. The checks are pure values, so
         they are UNIT tested over there - a checker nobody has watched reject anything is
         indistinguishable from one that accepts everything. Here the same checks run against a real
         machine. Copying the file would let the two drift, which is the whole failure this guards. -->
    <Compile Include="..\CcDirector.Core.Tests\Machine\ResourceSampleContract.cs" Link="Machine/ResourceSampleContract.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
